    "q-330821": {
      "dom_id": "step-q330821",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given that $L\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>3.$",
      "worked_solution": "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={e}^{3t}.$ Thus, for $s>3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s(s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
