    "q-330835": {
      "dom_id": "step-q330835",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2τ)\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$",
      "worked_solution": "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$ Thus, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
