    "q-330822": {
      "dom_id": "step-q330822",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{2}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$",
      "worked_solution": "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={t}^{2}.$ Thus, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2!}{{s}^{2+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{2}\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
