    "q-330838": {
      "dom_id": "step-q330838",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+4)},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+4)}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
